## Solution

Let box 1 contain $a$ marbles with $b$ black, and box 2 contain $c$ marbles with $d$ black. Since each box has both colors, $1 \le b \le a-1$ and $1 \le d \le c-1$, so $a,c \ge 2$.

We are given $a + c = 25$ and
$$\frac{b}{a}\cdot\frac{d}{c} = \frac{27}{50}, \quad\text{i.e.,}\quad 50bd = 27ac.$$

Since $\gcd(27,50)=1$, we need $50 \mid ac$.

**Enumerating $ac$ for $a+c=25$** (with $2 \le a \le 23$):

| $a$ | $c$ | $ac$ |
|-----|-----|------|
| 2 | 23 | 46 |
| 3 | 22 | 66 |
| 4 | 21 | 84 |
| 5 | 20 | **100** |
| 6 | 19 | 114 |
| 7 | 18 | 126 |
| 8 | 17 | 136 |
| 9 | 16 | 144 |
| 10 | 15 | **150** |
| 11 | 14 | 154 |
| 12 | 13 | 156 |

Only $ac = 100$ and $ac = 150$ are divisible by $50$ (the symmetric cases $a=20,c=5$ and $a=15,c=10$ are identical by swapping boxes).

**Case 1: $a=5, c=20$.** Then $bd = \frac{27\cdot100}{50}=54$, with $b\le 4$, $d\le 19$. Factor $54=1\cdot54=2\cdot27=3\cdot18=6\cdot9$. Only $b=3, d=18$ satisfies the bounds. So box 1 has 3 black, 2 white; box 2 has 18 black, 2 white.
$$P(\text{both white}) = \frac{2}{5}\cdot\frac{2}{20} = \frac{2}{5}\cdot\frac{1}{10} = \frac{1}{25}.$$

**Case 2: $a=10, c=15$.** Then $bd = \frac{27\cdot150}{50}=81$, with $b\le 9$, $d\le 14$. Factor $81=1\cdot81=3\cdot27=9\cdot9$. Only $b=9, d=9$ satisfies the bounds. So box 1 has 9 black, 1 white; box 2 has 9 black, 6 white.
$$P(\text{both white}) = \frac{1}{10}\cdot\frac{6}{15} = \frac{1}{10}\cdot\frac{2}{5} = \frac{1}{25}.$$

In every valid configuration, $P(\text{both white}) = \dfrac{1}{25}$. With $\gcd(1,25)=1$, we have $m=1, n=25$, so

$$m+n = 1+25 = \boxed{26}.$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
